ttyrec works only on BSD and old Linux.  It does rely on the ugly, 
problem-ridden ancient BSD-style pseudo-terminals, and support for those 
has been phased out of Linux in favour of better solutions.  You can still 
get them on 2.6 kernels, but that requires you to recompile your kernel 
with CONFIG_LEGACY_PTYS=y.

I have some code that does the appropiate autoconf magic well enough to 
make things working on all systems that smell like Unix, so this problem 
with ttyplay should be easy to fix.


I've done some research about this issue on Win32 as well, and it appears 
that this would be trickier to do.  Basically, unless I'm missing 
something, it's impossible to capture the full output of a Win32 native
(ie, not CygWin) console program if you want to get it in color.  However, 
we can just access the program's console every 0.01 second and write down 
the differences -- it's almost what we want, we'll just have a small 
chance to lose some text that was instantly dismissed by the player.




Another grudge I have with ttyrec/ttyplay is its format.  The recorded 
files are binary, and thus:

* are non-portable between architectures with different word size and/or 
  endianness.  IE, things recorded on a Mac can't be played on a PC and
  vice versa.

* it's pretty hard to tamper with the file using a text editor -- I like 
  to be able to trim the recording to get just a small piece of the best 
  stuff.

* you need special tools to convert the recorded file to your charset.
  This is the problem Mumon has.

Thus, I'm pondering using a format that uses ANSI codes (as in the 
devnull.net tournament) with timing information inserted using a made-up
"ESC [ nnn letter" code.  Of course, converting between this and ttyrec's 
format is trivial.




Another idea that does not require implementing a pseudo-terminal is to 
intercept the communication between you and a public NetHack server.  I've 
written something that could be modified for this task: 
http://jkm.dyndns.biz/logproxy/ (Win32-only).  I'm afraid you'll need to 
change the parameters and recompile it as I've deliberately hardcoded the
server used to dumb it down for some other users.  The current version 
does not record any timing information, but it would take just several
lines to add ttyrec-like data.




